{:db/id 17592186064204 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186064072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/id #uuid "e469c042-9619-55d4-b745-d4ed26251d80" :question/math-academy-id "e-447" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2}{(1-x)^3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2(1-x)^{-3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =  -\\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2(1-x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{u}^{-3}\\cdot (-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {u}^{-3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{-2}}{-2}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =(1-x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-2(1-x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186064480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2295f789-3426-5c13-8072-48ad046a0397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186250848} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4cefaf-cc26-5de6-9514-472253277759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186250939} {:answer/id #uuid "7672b2ad-aa8f-5d9b-b569-2cc387ccbabd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186251527} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2f7df9-5402-574e-adb2-2561ceb6b638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186252072} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e85065-6ff4-5195-b17d-22aae81082ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186252538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2295f789-3426-5c13-8072-48ad046a0397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186250848} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d653fa-fe23-5a9d-871d-2b6ab43cc543" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff0caf62-efde-5e81-b5d3-dad00511c8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-12019" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186064483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9923461c-ea68-5de4-a7c2-f232c840755f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (1)/(7(x - 3)^{7}) + C" :db/id 17592186061129} {:answer/id #uuid "a4dcfa8f-e3b7-5a31-89ee-98229564c8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(7(x - 3)^{7}) + C" :db/id 17592186061130} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bb9196-85e7-5244-8445-64c44a66da55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (1)/(7(x - 3)^{7})" :db/id 17592186061131} {:answer/id #uuid "5559a580-51a5-5468-9dd1-981105cf44fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(9(x - 3)^{9})" :db/id 17592186061132} {:answer/id #uuid "584255b1-754c-5fca-84ba-405b71b03c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(9(x - 3)^{9}) + C" :db/id 17592186061133}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ebeb1770-1d40-5b41-a8d0-c510eeac6052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186061128}] :question/id #uuid "fe72e74d-a333-5131-976a-24546ffabb73" :question/math-academy-id "q-12499" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int(1)/((x - 3)^{8})dx$."} {:db/id 17592186247360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc5c6fd-bdf8-52fc-bcc8-34f34365280e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5(5x-1)^{7}}+C" :db/id 17592186244989} {:answer/id #uuid "637d4b9b-93b2-5844-95c0-865fb0d8d6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{35(5x-1)^{7}}+C" :db/id 17592186245185} {:answer/id #uuid "a45fab6e-368a-5f8f-a84b-7e25d8888d84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5(5x-1)^{5}}+C" :db/id 17592186245784} {:answer/id #uuid "a986c32b-070a-5fc6-b027-801d97880cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}+C" :db/id 17592186245826} {:answer/id #uuid "b410e874-f38e-56b0-a2b2-4bb2712e9ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}+C" :db/id 17592186245917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a986c32b-070a-5fc6-b027-801d97880cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}+C" :db/id 17592186245826} :answer-field/id #uuid "865ffc4d-f1ea-5522-81fb-9f6d4c054a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a85bee8-3587-5c69-bd5f-2ededc7e8236" :question/math-academy-id "q-113511" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(5x-1)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x-1)^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x-1)^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-6}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}){u}^{-5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25{u}^{5}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-1)^{-5}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (5x-1)^{-6}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5(5x-1)^{6}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-1)^{6}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186247361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256fd45e-791f-579f-8818-27b200a5be2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6(2x+3)^{6}}+C" :db/id 17592186244646} {:answer/id #uuid "5af7f51f-ec62-58f7-bebf-0a5e46ab25ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}+C" :db/id 17592186245108} {:answer/id #uuid "86b0dab8-9a9c-5dc1-898f-16713fd82cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}+C" :db/id 17592186245500} {:answer/id #uuid "ba397c98-9bb5-5539-8012-42dae1cdb3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16(2x+3)^{8}}+C" :db/id 17592186245977} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bc3d02-224b-549e-92f2-90a5f845c0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(2x+3)^{8}}+C" :db/id 17592186246473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86b0dab8-9a9c-5dc1-898f-16713fd82cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}+C" :db/id 17592186245500} :answer-field/id #uuid "082ed3b9-9437-5ec7-963d-859b2b91ef1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "094b9ad8-d309-510f-9910-b086cac9a8d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-113512" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (2x+3)^{-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+3)^{-7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{-7}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}){u}^{-6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12{u}^{6}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x+3)^{-6}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-6)\\cdot (2x+3)^{-7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+3)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(2x+3)^{7}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{7}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186253660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee252b3-2fc9-58a1-a526-0ad834b9b6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186250686} {:answer/id #uuid "18391112-3f3a-57a1-9bfe-ef48a937491a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186250766} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9cae62-9c68-5e87-9549-65a72091d03a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186250813} {:answer/id #uuid "405c4f12-0925-5899-8b0c-bf237460ec93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186251088} {:answer/id #uuid "988c1baf-6f97-581c-b7f2-f8c57f43988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186251863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988c1baf-6f97-581c-b7f2-f8c57f43988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186251863} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae027c21-9aa0-573c-bcf0-1d5cd80234b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03a455ba-2873-5368-831e-308d6a1a251d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12018" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-3.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x-3)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-(-3)\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3)+0 \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186339111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0d85b8-3324-57f7-a467-f401be804083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186336578} {:answer/id #uuid "75e6122b-2104-52f4-b814-dae601247c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186337093} {:answer/id #uuid "8b91db05-c595-568e-b4ad-f97b5c3c6882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186337295} {:answer/id #uuid "aaed332c-7186-53b8-802e-6557581b75af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186337573} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ae619c-f25b-53f5-8b0f-30595a2ff518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186337926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a0d85b8-3324-57f7-a467-f401be804083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id 17592186336578} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda4e982-7475-54c5-abfe-c6e946514b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5692ce6-f75c-5a26-9487-0e066184decc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72429" :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-7.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -15{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-7)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-7)+0 \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186339113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c396ba-d664-5b85-8ba8-8e75dd8fbd90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186336119} {:answer/id #uuid "0db6811e-089b-562c-ba27-284a4fd3ea8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{(8-x)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186336160} {:answer/id #uuid "28d420b6-69c3-5606-bbbb-988485d9f4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(8-x)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186336414} {:answer/id #uuid "770e9c77-7b33-5f86-84b2-1c73fd57475c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(8-x)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186337104} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7a1e25-6e8c-5f9b-b884-a307ed2ee9c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186338106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c396ba-d664-5b85-8ba8-8e75dd8fbd90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186336119} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a548983-c429-59e9-a111-6bd915ce2c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e19e6ed6-2d0f-56db-a2d1-ee7e2496cc04" :question/math-academy-id "q-72431" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4}{(8-x)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 4(8-x)^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=8-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4(8-x)^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {u}^{-5}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)\\int {u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\int {u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{(-4)}{u}^{-4}+C \\\\  & =(8-x)^{-4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(8-x)^{-4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =(-4)(8-x)^{-5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-x)+0 \\\\  & =(-4)(8-x)^{-5}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4(8-x)^{-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(8-x)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186398109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87bb5e34-8a7e-5077-9056-44171c31d68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186398111} {:answer/id #uuid "73df82c1-76f6-50ea-99b9-53a33ba7607d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398112} {:answer/id #uuid "cfab3df7-4bb6-5eeb-9342-3841991827b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398113} {:answer/id #uuid "1e49762e-1e14-5248-8c9e-16a5e673bda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398114} {:answer/id #uuid "15a56691-9975-5ae2-aa7b-fda010fe63b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a56691-9975-5ae2-aa7b-fda010fe63b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398115} :answer-field/id #uuid "c13dea0f-f218-5cd2-a503-c7b2250d51fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186398110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1948aa2d-5fbb-59f5-8ccc-6dd9cfd6ef51" :question/math-academy-id "q-113509" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (x-2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(x-2)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (x-2)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-2)+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186398116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f0d601-5b9e-5b34-b1e4-35332ff61132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20(9-5x)^{4}}+C" :db/id 17592186398118} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0f063f-eba9-55db-a617-104bbe47e493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398119} {:answer/id #uuid "199a03cb-71ce-557c-b3af-135bd3564f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398120} {:answer/id #uuid "418bd3f4-e4c0-5bc7-b4ac-5541696af9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398121} {:answer/id #uuid "51978e0d-c725-5111-8fd8-58bfe89fa4d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "418bd3f4-e4c0-5bc7-b4ac-5541696af9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id 17592186398121} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b49b60e-8217-5844-bea9-fe48c12aaf25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186398117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d34fadec-e1db-5f0e-b3fb-64f3326f1376" :question/math-academy-id "q-113517" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(9-5x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=9-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}){u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(9-5x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (-2)(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(9-5x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(9-5x)}^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}
